Make zlib-ng usable from a build with no host tools on PATH, and let a
dependent include it with <angle brackets>.

Two unrelated problems in the BUILD file the registry adds.

The zconf.h genrule shells out to cp. A genrule runs with PATH pinned to
/bin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin, and a build that supplies its own tools rather
than the machine's has nothing there -- on NixOS the directories are empty
outright -- so it stops with "cp: command not found". The replacement is a
bash builtin and needs no PATH at all.

Separately, the file sets strip_include_prefix = "." and says it is there
because "Clang includes zlib with angled instead of quoted includes". It does
not achieve that: the target sits in its repository's root package, so the
prefix strips nothing, Bazel builds no virtual include directory, and the only
thing a dependent gets is the -iquote for the repository root it would have
had regardless. LLVM is one such dependent and fails to build against it:

    llvm/lib/Support/CRC.cpp:86:10: fatal error: zlib.h: No such file or
    directory

Adding `includes` puts the package on the system include path, which is what
the angled form needs.

--- a/BUILD.bazel
+++ b/BUILD.bazel
@@ -11,7 +11,13 @@
     name = "zconf_gen",
     srcs = ["zconf.h.in"],
     outs = ["zconf.h"],
-    cmd = "cp $(SRCS) $(OUTS)",
+    # A bash builtin rather than cp, so that this needs nothing on PATH: a
+    # genrule here runs with PATH pinned to /bin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin,
+    # which on a NixOS host is empty, and the point of this repository is that
+    # the tools a build runs are ones it built. `read -d ""` reads to the
+    # first NUL, which a text header does not contain, so it slurps the file
+    # whole -- trailing newlines and all -- and returns non-zero at EOF.
+    cmd = "IFS= read -r -d '' zconf < $(SRCS) || true; printf '%s' \"$$zconf\" > $(OUTS)",
 )
 
 cc_library(
@@ -80,5 +86,17 @@
     # Clang includes zlib with angled instead of quoted includes, so we need
     # strip_include_prefix here.
     strip_include_prefix = ".",
+    # ... except that strip_include_prefix on a target in its repository's
+    # root package strips nothing, so Bazel builds no virtual include
+    # directory and passes no -I. All the dependent gets is the -iquote for
+    # the repository root that it would have had anyway, which answers
+    # "zlib.h" and not <zlib.h>:
+    #
+    #     llvm/lib/Support/CRC.cpp:86:10: fatal error: zlib.h: No such file
+    #     or directory
+    #
+    # includes puts the package on the system include path, which is what the
+    # angled form needs. Both spellings then work.
+    includes = ["."],
     visibility = ["//visibility:public"],
 )
